# 5-Phase Plan: Guided Activity Protocol for CNS & Gait Analysis

## Background

The current system collects body pose data with a single generic instruction
("Stand naturally. Try not to shift your weight.") while running the SAME scan
for ALL biomarker systems simultaneously. This is fundamentally broken for CNS
and Skeletal because different biomarkers need the patient to do different things:

| Biomarker | Needs subject to… |
|---|---|
| Postural Entropy / Sway | Stand **perfectly still**, arms relaxed |
| Tremor (Welch PSD) | Hold arms slightly extended, **don't move** |
| Gait Variability (Zeni) | **Walk forward and back** (not in place) |
| Step Length Symmetry | Walk a few steps |
| Joint ROM | Slowly bend/extend arms and legs |
| Posture Alignment | Stand tall, look straight ahead |

Walking in place creates a periodic, predictable signal that is mathematically
identical to the "overly rigid posture" pattern of Parkinson's disease.

---

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Core Design Decision**: Should we run the body capture as **two sequential sub-phases** (still phase + walking phase), or should we **pick one and stick to it for the hackathon**?
>
> Option A (Full): Two sub-phases — ~8s still + ~8s walking. Richer data. More complex to implement.
>
> Option B (Simplified): One sub-phase (standing still). CNS and Skeletal both work stationary. Walking gait is "not assessed". Fastest to ship.
>
> **Recommendation: Option A** — but Phase 1 of this plan can be shipped independently without Phase 3.

> [!WARNING]
> The existing `body_capture_seconds = 8` config in `HardwareConfig` will need to be extended (to ~16s total) for the two sub-phase approach. This will increase total scan time.

